---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import DocHero from '../../components/DocHero.astro';

const pieces = [
	{
		href: '/documentation/looping-a-carousel',
		glyph: '∞',
		title: 'Looping a carousel that can’t be looped',
		dek: 'scrollLeft has a hard ceiling. Here’s how the Also Shipped carousel gets past it anyway — by giving itself somewhere further to go.',
	},
];
---

<Base title="Documentation — Jake Rochford" navLabel="Home" navHref="/" navIcon="pencil">
	<section class="doc-index">
		<div class="container doc-index__inner">
			<header class="doc-index__header">
				<h1 class="type-display">Documentation</h1>
				<p class="type-h3 doc-index__dek">How this site actually gets built</p>
			</header>

			<div class="doc-index__prose is-collapsed" id="doc-bio">
				<p class="type-body">
					I read a thread by Geoffrey Litt at Notion, based on a talk he'd given, and his argument
					stuck with me: as agents get better at writing and checking their own code, the bottleneck
					moves. It's not whether the code is correct anymore — it's whether the person working
					alongside the agent still understands what's being built. Not to verify it, but to stay
					fluent enough to make the next creative call, and to keep evolving the system instead of just
					accepting whatever it produces. His answer was{' '}
					<a href="https://gist.github.com/geoffreylitt/a29df1b5f9865506e8952488eac3d524" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>a prompt</a
					> he called <code>/explain-diff</code>,
					which turns a code change into a short explainer: background, an intuition-building
					walkthrough, the actual code, and — in his version — a short quiz gating whether you're
					allowed to ship.
				</p>
				<p class="type-body">
					I wanted to try a version of that here, but public. Partly to hold myself to actually staying
					informed about how this site gets built with an agent, and partly because I think that
					process is worth looking at in the open, not just the finished result. Every piece here
					follows Litt's shape — background, then an intuition, then the real code — written from an
					actual moment in building this site: a real bug, a real decision, an honest debugging story,
					in the site's own type and color system rather than a one-off file living outside the
					project. The one thing I dropped is the quiz — it was Litt's own speed regulator, for
					himself, mid-project, and it doesn't make much sense for someone dropping in from outside.
				</p>
				<p class="type-body">
					So this isn't a blog about the site. It's closer to a lab notebook that happens to be public
					— kept because staying legible to yourself, and to whoever's curious, is worth doing in the
					open.
				</p>
			</div>
			<button type="button" class="doc-index__read-more" id="doc-bio-toggle">Read more</button>

			<div class="doc-index__list">
				{pieces.map((piece) => (
					<a class="doc-card" href={piece.href}>
						<DocHero glyph={piece.glyph} label={`Cover image for “${piece.title}”`} />
						<div class="doc-card__body">
							<h2 class="type-h2">{piece.title}</h2>
							<p class="type-body doc-card__dek">{piece.dek}</p>
						</div>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>

			<p class="type-caption doc-index__note">More of these as they come up.</p>
		</div>

		<script>
			const prose = document.getElementById('doc-bio');
			const toggle = document.getElementById('doc-bio-toggle');

			toggle?.addEventListener('click', () => {
				prose?.classList.remove('is-collapsed');
				toggle.remove();
			});
		</script>
	</section>

	<style>
		.doc-index__inner {
			max-width: 680px;
			padding-block: var(--space-l);
		}

		.doc-index__dek {
			color: var(--color-ink-muted);
			font-weight: 400;
			margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
		}

		.doc-index__prose {
			position: relative;
			margin-block-start: var(--space-m);
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			gap: var(--space-s);
		}

		.doc-index__prose code {
			font-family: ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
			font-size: 0.9em;
			background: var(--color-panel);
			border-radius: 4px;
			padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
		}

		/* Collapsed by default so the explainer list below stays visible
		   without scrolling — the gradient signals there's more, "Read
		   more" removes the clamp (and itself) for good rather than
		   re-collapsing, since there's no reason to hide it again once
		   read. */
		.doc-index__prose.is-collapsed {
			max-height: 108px;
			overflow: hidden;
		}

		.doc-index__prose.is-collapsed::after {
			content: '';
			position: absolute;
			inset-inline: 0;
			bottom: 0;
			height: 3.5em;
			background: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, var(--color-bg));
			pointer-events: none;
		}

		.doc-index__read-more {
			display: inline-block;
			margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
			font-family: var(--font-text);
			font-size: var(--type-caption-size);
			color: var(--color-accent);
			text-decoration: underline;
			text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
			transition: opacity 0.15s ease;
		}

		.doc-index__read-more:hover,
		.doc-index__read-more:focus-visible {
			opacity: 0.7;
		}

		.doc-index__list {
			margin-block-start: var(--space-l);
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			gap: var(--space-s);
		}

		.doc-card {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			gap: var(--space-xs);
			background: var(--color-panel);
			border-radius: var(--radius-card);
			padding: var(--space-xs);
			transition: background-color 0.15s ease;
		}

		/* Overrides the global a:not(.button):hover underline rule —
		   without this, hovering the card underlines every line of text
		   in it, and even the hero image, since the decoration line
		   belongs to the anchor itself, not each piece of text inside
		   it. */
		.doc-card:hover,
		.doc-card:focus-visible {
			background: var(--color-line);
			text-decoration: none;
		}

		.doc-card__body {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			gap: var(--space-2xs);
			padding-inline: var(--space-2xs);
			padding-block-end: var(--space-2xs);
		}

		.doc-card__dek {
			color: var(--color-ink-muted);
		}

		.doc-index__note {
			margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
			color: var(--color-ink-muted);
		}
	</style>
</Base>
